+`stage-spec.operation` is owned by the selected producer closure's semantic
+interface. The provider author defines its logical operation tokens and their
+parameter/output meanings as part of that versioned interface; the host uses the
+exact token that interface defines. Tokens are not host-selected aliases,
+localized labels, or names for private physical planning choices. The same
+selected producer interface and logical operation use the same token bytes.
+Different providers may define different tokens: there is no platform-wide enum
+of stage operations, unlike `program-predicate.operation`, whose seven evaluator
+predicates are closed. Authoring a provider's interface is an explicit extension
+choice. The selected closure retains the implementation of that interface.
+Distinct logical operations of one interface take distinct tokens. The complete
+stage spec, including its Plan and output domains, determines `stageSpecDigest`;
+cache and regeneration keys commit that digest along with their other declared
+inputs. Two different acts cannot share identical complete committed inputs:
+there is no uncommitted operation discriminator. Changing an operation token
+changes stage identity and cache lookup. The host reproduces the token the
+selected interface defines; schema admission does not establish its meaning.
+Admission of `Text` establishes shape only; it does not establish that the
+producer supports an operation. Executing or replaying a stage must use the
+selected producer interface and its declared parameter/output contract.
+
+Both `outputDomains` arrays use the existing domain vocabulary in
+`identity-schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-digest-domains/byDomain`, enumerated by
+`#/$defs/Domain` and, for a typed `Ref`, by `#/$defs/Ref/properties/domain`;
+those two enumerations are the same registered set. Each member must be
+registered; the two arrays must still be equal. Agreement on an unregistered
+spelling does not admit it. An empty array is permitted and declares no output
+domains; it establishes no facts, Coverage, successful predicate, or authority
+by itself. Every actual
+output remains subject to the selected producer interface, output schema,
+protocol and retained-evidence laws. Declaring a domain grants no permission to
+produce or consume a record otherwise forbidden by those laws.
+
+`plan.semanticClosures` records the Plan's explicit selection, with a required
+minimum rather than an exact minimal set. For evaluated views, the producing
+closure and each scope's enumerating closure must be selected; a member fact's
+producer must equal its view's producer. Selected stage specs and cache keys
+bound to the Plan likewise name selected producers. The evaluation seal's
+evaluator must be selected and the proof bundle must name that same evaluator.
+Each evaluated finding's rule closure must be selected. The
+`x-opensip-digest-domains.closureMembership` annotation states these bindings
+beside the existing closure-kind rules.
+
+An import's producer and adapter are retained through the selected `import2`
+input; native toolchain, standard-library, LLVM and grammar closures are retained
+through the selected native contexts and their registered closure joins. They
+need not also be direct `semanticClosures` members merely because those selected
+inputs depend on them. They may be selected explicitly, and a Plan may select
+other admissible retained closures. Changing that selection changes the Plan's
+semantic inputs and identity. This does not turn unreferenced CAS blobs into
+evaluation inputs or remove any direct membership or retention obligation.
